# The verification surface

Stages 3–4. This is the product — the screen the pilot actually lives in.
Everything here is judged by one number: median pharmacist seconds-per-script
at verification, against Anjali's Week-1 baseline.

## Two lanes

- **All-green → fast lane.** One tap to accept. **Never zero-touch** — there
  is no code path that accepts a record without an explicit pharmacist action.
- **Any amber or red → review lane**, sorted by severity, focus auto-landing
  on the riskiest field rather than the top of the form.

## Tier-driven UX

- **Green** — pre-checked and quiet. Don't make the pharmacist confirm what
  the system is confident about; that's the time saving.
- **Amber** — requires explicit acknowledgment.
- **Red** — blocks the fast path outright.

Tiers arrive precomputed on the `VerificationRecord`. The UI renders them;
it never derives or overrides them.

## Side-by-side viewer

Original image / PDF (pdf.js) beside the populated form, with a
**bidirectional** bounding-box overlay: click a field → highlight its source
region, click a region → focus its field. This is what makes correcting
faster than retyping, which is the whole value proposition. Treat it as core,
not polish.

## Pre-checks

Allergy-vs-profile, duplicate-vs-history, dose plausibility, refill-too-soon,
out-of-stock — run against the Liberty profile before the pharmacist looks.

**Additive only.** Liberty's own clinical/DUR engine remains authoritative at
final verification. Never present a pre-check as a cleared interaction check,
and never suppress a Liberty warning because our pre-check passed.

## Correction logging

Every field edit emits a `CorrectionEvent`. Append-only — never UPDATE or
DELETE. This is simultaneously the compliance trail and Ananth's calibration
signal, so a dropped correction is a dropped test case.

## Data layer

- Postgres-backed queue table, **polling via TanStack Query**. No websockets
  until staleness is an actual observed complaint.
- PHI-bearing reads go through the data layer, never raw SQL in a route handler.
- Record IDs in URLs. Never a patient name, DOB, or Rx detail in a query
  string or path param.

## Metrics dashboard

Seconds-per-script (headline), auto-accept rate, corrections by field, flags
caught — each compared against the Week-1 baseline.
